1175 runs, 28 agent_model, 26 agent families

=== 1. can the agent be recovered from a column the mask leaves behind? ===
          column  levels   purity   single-agent groups  verdict
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      experiment      58   100.0%         58/58         IS agent identity
        run_name    1175   100.0%       1175/1175       IS agent identity
    trace_format       4    32.3%          1/4          weak
       benchmark       7    13.6%          0/7          weak
   trained_model       4    13.4%          0/4          weak

=== 2. shipped split: every parameter-free lookup, with and without a mask ===
            baseline                               keys  regret@3      rho
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         global-mean                             (none)    0.1543     +nan
           per-agent                        agent_model    0.0000  +0.7507  masked away
    per-agent-family                       agent_family    0.0000  +0.7514  masked away
 per-agent×benchmark              agent_model+benchmark    0.0000  +0.7507  masked away
      per-agent×cell agent_model+benchmark+trained_model    0.1543     +nan  masked away
      per-experiment                         experiment    0.0000  +0.7080
          per-format                       trace_format    0.0844  +0.4029

  best lookup that reads no agent column: per-experiment -> regret@3 0.0000, rho +0.7080
  48/50 test rows share an experiment with train, and every experiment is one agent, so the
  saturating lookup survives the mask unchanged. Masking is not a substitute
  for holding the family out -- and `experiment` and `run_name` belong on the
  excluded-columns list next to the post-execution fields.

=== 3. the confound no split and no mask can fix ===
  58 experiments, all single-agent: 'which agent' and 'which recipe'
  are the same variable in 1175/1175 rows. The corpus is nested,
  not crossed. Only new rollouts that cross the two separate them.

  Incidental crossing that already exists, on the 143 extracted recipes:
  12/49 pipeline signatures were produced by more than one agent family.

                pipeline signature  agents  acc spread
                               sft      19      0.8036
                         sft>merge       9      0.6679
                           sft>sft       7      0.4261
                       sft>package       7      0.6027
                       sft>sft>sft       4      0.1941
                           rft>sft       3      0.2244
                          sft>grpo       3      0.2919
                     sft>grpo>grpo       3      0.0743

  The algorithm family is nearly uninformative: 19 agents ran plain `sft` and
  landed 0.80 of accuracy apart. Whatever signal exists is in the detail the
  extraction currently discards, or in execution quality.
